 "pattern": "Hash map + doubly linked list",
 "statement": "Design a cache that holds at most `capacity` keys. `get(key)` returns the value or −1. `put(key, value)` inserts or updates; if that would exceed the capacity, the **least recently used** key is evicted first. Both operations must run in O(1).",
 "example": {
  "input": "LRUCache(2)\nput(1, 10)\nput(2, 20)\nget(1)  →  10\nput(3, 30)\nget(2)  →  -1\nget(3)  →  30",
  "output": "",
  "why": "Reading key 1 made it recently used, so when key 3 arrived it was key 2 that got evicted."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "LRUCache(2)\nput(1, 1)\nput(2, 2)\nget(1)  →  1\nput(3, 3)\nget(2)  →  -1\nput(4, 4)\nget(1)  →  -1\nget(3)  →  3\nget(4)  →  4",
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "input": "LRUCache(1)\nput(2, 1)\nput(2, 5)\nget(2)  →  5\nput(3, 9)\nget(2)  →  -1",
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "picture": "Picture a small shelf that holds only `capacity` books, and a list of those books in order of use, the most recently used at the front. Reading or adding a book moves it to the front of the list. When a new book needs room on a full shelf, the book at the back of the list, unused for the longest, has to go. A dictionary finds any book instantly, and the linked list makes moving a book to the front instant too.",
 "idea": [
  "A hash map gives O(1) lookup, but it has no idea which key was used longest ago.",
  "A **doubly linked list** can keep the keys ordered by use: most recent at the front, least recent at the back. Moving a node to the front or removing the last one is O(1) — if we can find the node instantly.",
  "So combine them: the map finds things in O(1), the list keeps the order. Every `get` and `put` moves its key to the front; eviction removes the back."
 ],
 "code": "class LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = {}\n        self.order = DoublyLinkedList()\n\n    def get(self, key: int) -> int:\n        if key in self.cache:\n            self.order.move_to_front(key)\n            return self.cache[key]\n        return -1\n\n    def put(self, key: int, value: int) -> None:\n        if key in self.cache:\n            self.cache[key] = value\n            self.order.move_to_front(key)\n        else:\n            if len(self.cache) >= self.capacity:\n                removed_key = self.order.remove_last()\n                del self.cache[removed_key]\n            self.cache[key] = value\n            self.order.add_to_front(key)\n\nclass DoublyLinkedList:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.head = ListNode()\n        self.tail = ListNode()\n        self.head.next = self.tail\n        self.tail.prev = self.head\n        self.nodes = {}\n\n    def add_to_front(self, key):\n        node = ListNode(key)\n        node.next = self.head.next\n        node.prev = self.head\n        self.head.next.prev = node\n        self.head.next = node\n        self.nodes[key] = node\n\n    def move_to_front(self, key):\n        node = self.nodes[key]\n        node.prev.next = node.next\n        node.next.prev = node.prev\n        self.add_to_front(key)\n\n    def remove_last(self):\n        node = self.tail.prev\n        node.prev.next = self.tail\n        self.tail.prev = node.prev\n        del self.nodes[node.key]\n        return node.key\n\nclass ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, key=None):\n        self.key = key\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None",
